    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a2346a1a-7122-50ef-ba01-6ff7038ca65e",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x y}{2}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/review-0-policy-2-4c6625aaf57ad834/runtime/package/17592186172040/plots/1e987f302652c692c3b7d97444f651f43bc97c07c2b1b6a61f69c12e7cde00aa.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/review-0-policy-2-4c6625aaf57ad834/runtime/package/17592186172040/plots/8b2ecd27e22d74c5785364a329965c12399ff917763b327028d8b8eecadc8808.png)",
